    "body": "### Question Review\nSince email fields are mostly just text fields, why doesn’t HTML allow selecting text inside them with setSelectionRange() like normal text inputs?\n\n### User Experiencing\n`<input type=\"email\" id=\"mail\">`\n`document.getElementById(\"mail\").setSelectionRange(0, 5);`\n\nBrowser throws :\nInvalidStateError\n\n### Confusion \nEmail input is basically text only \nSo , normal text inputs allow text selection , why email does not.\n\n### Because\ninput[type=email] is intentionally excluded from setSelectionRange() by the HTML Standard. The API only applies to text, search, url, tel, and password. Although email inputs appear text-like, they are treated as specialized semantic controls and may represent tokenized or browser-enhanced values (especially with multiple). This is why browsers throw InvalidStateError instead of allowing selection APIs.\n\n### Solution 1\nChange Type=\"email\" --> type = \"text\"\nUse\n`<input type=\"text\" id=\"mail\" inputmode=\"email\">`\nthen \n`const input = document.getElementById(\"mail\");\ninput.setSelectionRange(0, 5);`\n\nwhy input mode email\nit gives email keyboard on mobile \nuser firendly typing experience\n\n### Solution 2 \nkeep type =\"email\" but avoid selection API\n`<input type=\"email\">`\nthe dont use setSelectionRange()\ninstead \n`input.focus()`",
